WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.199
Film Courage: Well, Joe you’ve covered so
many niches from [the rock band] Metallica

00:00:03.199 --> 00:00:08.230
in therapy, the [Arkansas] West Memphis 3,
[author/coach] Tony Robbins, [Boston ] Whitey

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:13.150
B., at this stage of your career how do you
know when you want to invest time and money

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:14.389
into a story?

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:18.420
What is it about a story where you say “Okay,
this is something I want to commit several

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:22.050
years and maybe even a lifetime to dealing
with?”

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:26.680
Joe: You know, I’ve been lucky enough to
cover a wide range of material and to make

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:29.160
quite a few films.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.780
Somehow they keep letting me do it.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:40.149
And the strange thing is that sometimes instead
of me choosing the subject, the subject kind

00:00:40.149 --> 00:00:47.150
of chooses me at a period if my life where
I feel like there is something I need to learn.

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:50.880
For Tony Robbins [I AM NOT YOUR GURU] , I
was open to that kind of thinking for the

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.720
first time in my life and it’s a very positive
film because Tony had a very positive impact

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:58.079
on my life and I wanted to share that with
other people.

00:00:58.079 --> 00:01:03.640
You know, some people looked at that film
and were waiting for the third-act-shoe-to-

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:08.850
drop where I ripped him to shreds and show
why what he’s doing is a bad thing, but

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:10.779
that wasn’t my intention.

00:01:10.779 --> 00:01:17.049
With Metallica, there was a whole other set
of reasons in my life why that film came to

00:01:17.049 --> 00:01:18.049
me.

00:01:18.049 --> 00:01:25.420
So when I look back (in some ways and a lot
of the times) the subject that I need to learn

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:31.729
about at that moment seems to be my guiding
principle (as odd as that sounds) but it’s

00:01:31.729 --> 00:01:33.429
not just a passive thing either.

00:01:33.429 --> 00:01:38.179
Obviously I choose the films that I want to
make and generally I look for opportunities

00:01:38.179 --> 00:01:47.169
where by focusing on the plight of a particular
individual it shines a light on a larger aspect

00:01:47.169 --> 00:01:50.049
of the human condition.

00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:57.280
And I think film is an incredible medium for
social change.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:03.739
I mean I’ve had the good fortune of where
much of my work has had a direct impact on

00:02:03.739 --> 00:02:06.659
a case or a situation.

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:15.939
And so I just finished a couple of recent
television series, one for Spike.

00:02:15.939 --> 00:02:23.069
It’s called ‘Gone, The Forgotten Women
of Ohio,‘ where the mothers of these missing

00:02:23.069 --> 00:02:27.400
women were feeling like nobody cared about
their situation, nobody cared about solving

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.450
this situation against these women because
they were judged by society.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:39.319
And I felt like look, everyone deserves justice
regardless of your lifestyle and so I looked

00:02:39.319 --> 00:02:47.280
into that case and was compelled to try and
help and provide some answers and I felt I

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.189
could actually make a difference.

00:02:49.189 --> 00:02:53.910
Some stories are presented to me where I feel
like I can’t get the access or whatever.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:03:03.079
But you know, it’s a strange group of criteria
that make me feel like this is the right thing.

00:03:03.079 --> 00:03:08.600
And then at the most basic level, is this
a situation or are these people that I want

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:15.939
to spend two, two and a half years with because
that’s an arc of a film.

00:03:15.939 --> 00:03:21.730
And even if it’s an ugly story, whether
it’s a pleasant story or an ugly story,

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:27.450
that’s not the deciding factor of ‘Do
I want to spend two, two and a half years?’

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:29.840
It’s who are these people?

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.239
Can I make an impact?

00:03:32.239 --> 00:03:35.769
Can I be successful in my filmmaking mission?

00:03:35.769 --> 00:03:40.709
And those are kind of the factors as to whether
or not you want to spend two and a half years

00:03:40.709 --> 00:03:41.900
of your life doing something.

